# Abstract 

Species names provide the foundation for organizing biological knowledge, linking information across evolution, conservation, ecologygenomics, epidemiology, and biodiversity monitoring. Yet the taxonomic concepts associated with those names often change through time, creating challenges for interpreting and integrating data collected under different classifications. Bats, the second most diverse order among mammals, provide a useful model system for examining these challenges because they have undergone extensive taxonomic revision in recent decades and increasingly serve as focal taxa in research ranging from conservation to disease ecology. To address this challenge, we developed the Bat Taxonomic Alignment (BTA), a framework that reconciles taxonomic concepts across ten fifteen major bat authorities spanning 45 years (1980–2025). The BTA enables users to track how species delimitationsconcepts have changed through time, linking historical and contemporary classifications while preserving provenance and alternative treatments. Application of the framework revealed substantial disagreement among contemporary taxonomic authorities, with hundreds of conflicting species treatments persisting despite increasing efforts toward standardization. To demonstrate its utility, we used the BTA to audit taxonomic consistency within the IUCN Red List, identifying 287 taxonomic needed modifications, affecting \~20% of assessed bat species, and demonstrating how taxonomic discrepancies can propagate into conservation infrastructure. Although developed using bats as a model system, the challenges addressed by the BTA extend across biodiversity science wherever changing taxonomies influence the interpretation of biological data. By making taxonomic concepts explicit, traceable, and interoperable, the framework supports more transparent, reproducible, and integrated use of biological data.

**Keywords:** Taxonomic concepts; Taxonomic alignment; Biodiversity informatics; Biodiversity data integration; Species concepts

# Introduction 

Taxonomy is the primary indexing system of biological knowledge. Taxonomic names link data from field observations to museum specimens, genomic sequences to ecological interactions, hosts to pathogen records, and conservation status to legal protections. Across disciplines, species names function as the keys by which data are organized, integrated, and interpreted. Effective research, management, and policy depend on clearly defined taxonomic units that reflect biological reality and can be tracked through space and time (Voigt et al 2016; Lyal et al. 2008; Sharma et al. 2024). In this sense, taxonomy is not merely a classificatory exercise; it is the foundational infrastructure for biodiversity science, public health, and environmental governance (Abrahamse, 2021CITE CITE).

The global taxonomic infrastructure operates under increasing strain. Global biodiversity is declining at an unprecedented rate (Keck et al. 2025), intensifying the need for accurate species identification and tracking, while biological data are being generated, digitized, and aggregated at unprecedented scales (Bayraktarov et al., 2019; Heberling et al., 2021; Ji et al., 2026; GBIF No. 12, 2025). Occurrence records, genomic sequences, ecological networks, and host–pathogen databases – to give some examples \- are increasingly integrated across global repositories such as the Global Biodiversity Information Facility (GBIF), GenBank, BOLD, and institutional collections. The reliability of these systems depends on the assumption that taxonomic names consistently refer to comparable biological entities. Increasingly, that assumption does not hold.

A central but often overlooked issue is that a name string \- the sequence of letters used to represent the name of a biological taxon (e.g., *Homo sapiens*) \- is not equivalent to the taxonomic species concept it represents. Taxonomic names are shorthand for evolving hypotheses about species boundaries and evolutionary relationships (Zachos, 2018). The same binomial may refer to different circumscriptions – the set of populations included in a taxon \- across taxonomic classifications or time, while different names may refer to the same lineage. Advances in integrative taxonomy increasingly combine molecular, morphological, ecological, behavioural, and biogeographic evidence to refine species boundaries and classifications (Dayrat, 2005; Padial et al., 2010; Solari et al., 2019). As a result, species concepts are continually reassessed as new evidence emerges (de Queiroz, 2025; Zachos, 2018). Differences in synonymy, rank, genus placement, spelling, or species delimitation therefore reflect scientific progress, yet biodiversity data systems frequently treat names as stable identifiers (Page 2008). Without explicit tracking of taxonomic species concepts, including who defined them, when, and under what circumscription, data repositories implicitly assume stability. As a result, records may be combined or split based solely on name matching, rather than if they represent the same biological concept.

Because scientific names underpin biological databases, museum collections, scientific publications, genomic resources, and conservation assessments, changes in species circumscriptions can propagate throughout interconnected biological infrastructures and downstream applications (Figure 1).

