Despite the centrality of taxonomy to many scientific disciplines, there remains no widely available framework that transparently aligns competing taxonomic concepts across authorities and time. A range of tools exists to reconcile taxonomic name strings across datasets, including fuzzy matching algorithms (e.g., Taxamatch; Rees, 2014), web-based resolution services (e.g., TNRS; Boyle et al., 2013), multi-database query frameworks (Berendsohn et al. 2026), and phylogeny matching tools (e.g., Phylomatcher; Radar et al., 2023). These approaches are effective for correcting misspellings, resolving orthographic variants, and identifying synonyms, but they operate primarily at the level of name strings rather than dynamic taxonomic concepts (Tsang et al., 2016). Consequently, they do not resolve deeper incongruences arising from alternative species circumscriptions, rank changes, or conflicting taxonomic treatments (Franz et al., 2016; Patterson et al., 2010). Large biodiversity infrastructures such as GBIF maintain extensive synonymy and cross-reference records, yet their effective use still requires manual interpretation to determine which taxonomic concept a name actually represents. 

Even in the largest data repositories, significant ingestion gaps persist (Garnett, 2020). When aggregators attempt to harmonize data from hundreds of sources, they frequently adopt a single preferred taxonomic treatment (Mesibov, 2013; 2018; Franz et al., 2016). As demonstrated by Mesibov (2013, 2018), automated look-up tables often prioritize string-to-string matching over taxonomic concepts, potentially overwriting the original taxonomic intent of source datasets. Consequently, records may be reassigned to accepted names that reflect different taxonomic concepts or an outdated circumscription (Burgin et al., 2018). Without provenance or concept-level tracking, it becomes difficult for researchers to determine whether a name reflects a contemporary taxonomic consensus or simply an artifact of an automated harmonization process.

As taxonomic discovery accelerates and global data integration expands, the need for transparent, versioned alignment of taxonomic concepts becomes increasingly urgent. Currently, no freely available framework provides concept-driven reconciliation of taxonomic names across taxonomic authorities while simultaneously preserving alternative interpretations, historical provenance, and the temporal and geographic context in which taxonomic decisions are made (Radar et al., 2023). 

Bats (Order Chiroptera) provide an ideal system in which to develop and test a concept alignment framework. The number of recognized bat species has increased by more than one-third since the early 1990s, reflecting rapid advances in phylogenetics, integrative taxonomy, and the resolution of cryptic species complexes (Monadjem et al., 2024; Burgin et al., 2025). As illustrated in Figure 1, taxonomic revisions can substantially alter the biological meaning associated with scientific names, creating challenges for many downstream disciplines, including biodiversity monitoring, conservation assessments, ecological studies, ecosystem service evaluations, and disease surveillance. Although bats are used here as a model system, these challenges extend across biodiversity science wherever data are linked through taxonomic names that change through time.

Here, we present the Bat Taxonomic Alignment (BTA), an open-access, version-controlled framework designed to document and align taxonomic concepts across major taxonomic authorities spanning more than four decades. Rather than acting as a new taxonomic authority, the BTA preserves and organizes how species concepts are treated across alternative classifications, explicitly recording synonymy, rank changes, genus transfers, and competing interpretations. By shifting the focus from name strings to taxonomic concepts, the BTA provides a transparent bridge among biodiversity repositories, literature, and conservation workflows. Using bats as a globally relevant case study, we demonstrate how concept-level alignment can reconcile alternative taxonomic treatments, quantify disagreement among leading taxonomic authorities, and reveal inconsistencies in conservation infrastructure through an audit of the IUCN Red List. Together, these analyses illustrate the broader value of taxonomic concept alignments for accurate and reproducible biodiversity science, conservation priorities, and biodiversity data integration in an era of rapid taxonomic change.

# The Bat Taxonomic Alignment (BTA) Tool

The Bat Taxonomic Alignment (BTA) was developed through a three-stage workflow comprising the acquisition and digitization of taxonomic authorities, alignment of taxonomic concepts across classifications, and dissemination of the resulting resource as a version-controlled, open-access dataset (Figure 2). Detailed descriptions of each component of the framework are provided below. Definitions of key taxonomic concept alignment terms are provided in Supplementary 1\.

![][image2]

**Figure 2\. Workflow of the Bat Taxonomic Alignment**. The BTA integrates taxonomic treatments from historical and contemporary authorities, reconciles taxonomic concepts through a combination of automated matching and expert review, and disseminates the resulting concept-alignment framework as a versioned, open-access resource. Historical and legacy authorities were digitized and standardized alongside contemporary digital taxonomies, enabling taxonomic concepts to be linked across 45 years of bat systematics. The resulting framework preserves provenance, documents concept relationships, and supports FAIR (Findable, Accessible, Interoperable, and Reusable) dissemination through versioned releases on Zenodo.

**Taxonomic scope and authority coverage**

The Bat Taxonomic Alignment (BTA) was developed as a proof-of-concept framework for documenting and reconciling taxonomic concepts across changing classifications and through time. Bats provide an ideal system in which to develop and test such a framework because they have experienced extensive taxonomic revision over recent decades, resulting in substantial changes to species circumscriptions across major taxonomic authorities. Because bats underpin research and decision-making across fields ranging from conservation and biodiversity monitoring to disease ecology and public health, these changes can have far-reaching consequences. Although the BTA wasis developed using bats as a model system, the underlying challenge of reconciling changing taxonomic concepts extends throughout biodiversity science.

To capture this dynamic history, the BTA incorporates taxonomic treatments from tenfifteen major authorities, some represented by multiple versions, spanning 45 years (1980–2025; Table 1). Authorities were selected to represent major milestones in the development of bat taxonomy, including globally recognized taxonomic references, conservation taxonomies, and contemporary digital taxonomic resources. These include successive editions of Mammal Species of the World (MSW), Handbook of the Mammals of the World (HMW), BatNames, the Mammal Diversity Database (MDD), and the IUCN Red List, together with additional authorities that have played important roles in shaping contemporary bat taxonomy. Collectively, these works provide a chronological record of changing species circumscriptions, synonymies, genus assignments, and higher-level classifications, documenting a period during which recognized bat diversity increased from fewer than 1,000 species in the early 1980s to 1,498 species in 2025\.

