A key feature of the BTA is the preservation of provenance. Rather than replacing historical classifications with a single preferred taxonomy, the framework retains authority-specific treatments together with their associated taxonomic notes, source citations, and supporting information. Complete verbatim treatments are preserved within the dataset, enabling users to examine the original context in which alignmenttaxonomic decisions were made and to trace the evidence supporting changes in species circumscriptions through time. This allows users to determine, for example, when a species concept was first recognized, which historical concepts contributed to a contemporary species, how different authorities interpreted species limits, and what taxonomic evidence or geographic scope underpinned those decisions. By maintaining direct links to the original treatments, the BTA provides a transparent record of taxonomic reasoning rather than only the final taxonomic outcome.

The resulting framework functions as an alignment layer among taxonomic authorities rather than a replacement taxonomy. By documenting concept relationships, preserving provenance, and retaining alternative names and treatments, the BTA enables biodiversity data collected under different classifications to be interpreted within an explicit taxonomic context. Detailed descriptions of concept matching procedures, relationship definitions, and manual reconciliation examples are provided in Supplementary 3\.

**Resource format and accessibility**

The BTA is distributed as a version-controlled, open-access resource on Zenodo (Sherman et al., 2025\) in both human-readable and machine-readable formats. It can be accessed at: https://zenodo.org/records/16423149XXX. Each version release receives a persistent DOI, enabling users to cite the exact version used in analyses while ensuring that previous versions remain permanently accessible.

The BTA is a spreadsheet organized around species concepts and consists of four primary components: (i) Valid Names, an interpreted index of species concepts across authorities; (ii) Full Treatments, which preserve verbatim authority-specific taxonomic accounts and supporting notes; (iii) Alternative Names, a comprehensive index of synonyms, subspecies, misspellings, abbreviations, and historical name variants; and (iv) concept relationship tables documenting how species concepts change across authorities and through time. An overview of the column layout and contents is summarized in Table 2\.

